  "question": {
    "text": "A geostationary satellite above the equator is orbiting around the earth at a fixed distance $r_1$ from the center of the earth. A second satellite is orbiting in the equatorial plane in the opposite direction to the earth's rotation, at a distance $r_2$ from the center of the earth, such that $r_1=1.21 r_2$. The time period of the second satellite as measured from the geostationary satellite is $\\frac{24}{p}$ hours. The value of $p$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2.33",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_advanced/jKpQ6HTfHtrSqMo-D_gFNvnqR3tEyJWKvMUd-dwYpQo.original.fullsize.png\"><br>$\\mathrm{T} \\propto \\mathrm{r}^{3 / 2}$\n$\\frac{\\mathrm{T}_2}{\\mathrm{~T}_1}=\\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{r}_2}{\\mathrm{r}_1}\\right)^{3 / 2}$\n$\\frac{\\omega_2}{\\omega_1}=\\left(\\frac{r_1}{r_2}\\right)^{3 / 2}=(1.21)^{3 / 2}$\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\omega_2=\\omega_1(1.331) \\qquad ...(i)\\\\\n& \\left(\\omega_2+\\omega_1\\right) t_0=2 \\pi \\qquad ...(ii)\n\\end{aligned}$\n$t_0=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\omega_2+\\omega_1}=\\frac{2 \\pi}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}+1\\right) \\omega_1}=\\frac{6 \\pi}{7 \\omega_1}$\n$\\mathrm{t}_0=\\frac{6 \\pi}{2 \\pi} \\frac{\\left(\\mathrm{~T}_{\\mathrm{GSS}}\\right)}{(7)}$\n$\\mathrm{t}_0=\\frac{3 \\times 24 \\text { hours }}{7}=\\frac{24}{\\mathrm{p}} \\text { hours }$\n$\\mathrm{p}=\\frac{7}{3}=2.33$",
    "image": null
  },
